# ASC: go wild, the minimal way

This is a **garage** project. Explore. Break a frozen checklist when a smaller or truer thing appears. Go wild **the minimal way**: fewest files, fewest always-sourced lines, no completeness theater.

**Trust:** every runtime claim is a **literal ASC test** (`make test-core`, `asc/test/**/*.test.sh`, or the `$subject-$action` under test). Unrun stays **unverified**. Do not invent a pass.

**Every step, every level:** smallest durable upgrade to **this rule** and/or `changelog/`. Skip if already here or one-off. Never a new `.mdc`, loader, hook, or wrapper as the memory vehicle. Facts true only for one project instance stay in that instance (`scripts/asc/extend/`). Knowledge/task **gap** types are not core (README Non-goals). Do not add `gap.entity.yml` / `gap.able.yml` in this tree.

Apply when this workspace is ASC, the tree has `asc/bootstrap.sh`, or the task touches such a tree. Otherwise skip.

## Root `README.md` (human, single source of truth)

`README.md` at this repo root is the **only** source of truth for what ASC is. The human writes it. Changelog, plans, tests, and this rule are working notes: they must not silently override it.

When README is wrong, incomplete, internally inconsistent, or in conflict with code / this rule: **say so in the chat, every time**. Do not paper over it. Do not treat `changelog/` as a competing SoT. Do not dump the long form into README to “make it true.”

Agents may edit `README.md` **only** inside dated proposal delimiters. Use **today’s** date. Do not replace, delete, or rewrite any human-written line. The proposal may only sit **next to** the line(s) to improve.

Write the tags **escaped** in README source (`&lt;` / `&gt;`). Raw `<proposal-…>` is HTML; GitHub will hide it. Each delimiter tag has a blank line immediately above and below it.

```markdown
human-written line

&lt;proposal-2026-09-21&gt;

proposed wording

&lt;/proposal-2026-09-21&gt;

```

Each proposal must make the nearby human text **lighter or clearer**, not larger. One example per idea. No plan dumps, no hypothetical files, no exhaustive matrices.

```text
❌ Replace or delete a human-written README line
❌ Raw <proposal-YYYY-MM-DD> (unescaped; GitHub hides it)
❌ Tag with no blank line immediately above or below it
❌ Edit README outside &lt;proposal-YYYY-MM-DD&gt; … &lt;/proposal-YYYY-MM-DD&gt;
❌ changelog/YYYY/MM/DD-readme-<slug>.md, README.* siblings, or .diff files
❌ Paste a 20-row loader table “so every case is covered”
❌ “Changelog is the SoT; README can wait”
✅ Point out the mismatch in chat
✅ Insert an escaped, blank-line-wrapped &lt;proposal-YYYY-MM-DD&gt; block next to the human line
✅ Changelog / docs / plans for the long form (not README)
```

## Any ASC tree (core, ext, contrib, instance)

Ship the smallest thing that still works. Prefer fewer files, fewer always-sourced lines, fewer wrappers.

Two include kinds only: `*.inc.sh` (eager) and `*.opt-inc.sh` (lazy). No third suffix (`private-inc`, …). Kernel subject dir is `asc/core/`. Kernel utils hub is `asc/core/utils.inc.sh` (hardcoded `.` in bootstrap). `asc/core/utils/` is not a caller or hook dir — `*.opt-inc.sh` there is never auto; callers `.` it.

```text
❌ New include “for completeness” with no caller
❌ Eager *.inc.sh for a function one action/hook needs
❌ Clone a sibling’s `. foo.inc.sh` when this file never calls it
❌ Document or create a file that does not exist yet
❌ Third include suffix so utils “looks different”
❌ Knowledge/task `gap` entity in core
✅ Match an existing script’s *shape that still runs*; drop unused sources
✅ Lazy or in-entry-point only after a real caller exists
```

A sibling’s source line is **not** a caller in this file. Example: `caller_opt_inc.test.sh` is bootstrap + shunit2 — not `test.inc.sh` (that was `primitives.test.sh`; and `asc/test/test.inc.sh` is already eager). A human “why is this `.` here?” is a patch to **this rule** and/or `changelog/` when the include has no caller. Skip if one-off.

Ask before adding a loader, hook, wrapper, or global. Changelog may be long; README and runtime paths may not.

## Labels (human ↔ token)

A **label** is a **self-explainable name**: it says what the thing does without a footnote. `caller opt-inc` is a label; `phase 90` is not (that was a numbered filename; the code now lives at the tail of `asc/bootstrap.sh`).

The human writes French, English, or Brazilian Portuguese. **English** is the **pivot NL** in this tree: the canonical natural-language spelling of a label (not a make `$subject-$action` pivot). FR and PT-BR are redundant routes onto that same English label, then onto the token. A later **Projet Complexe** instance may declare its own counterpart; that stays in that instance, not here.

Natural language is redundant; human memory is **disparate** (nicknames, near-misses, three languages). Machine concepts need to stay more or less **deterministic** (tokens, loaders, make pivots, harnesses).

A **harness** is generic ASC-level hook/pivot vs contrib-level specificity — not a test runner. One abstract `$subject-$action`; contrib is the `hook_ms` winner. They share the same `*.entity.yml` / `*.able.yml` contracts. Do not mint a parallel pivot or a second entity spec per tool. Shape: `agent-swarm-start` (generic) vs `cursor-swarm-start` / `codex-swarm-start` (contrib). Those swarm names are **not** files in this tree. Same law on disk: `make agent-start` → `hook_ms -s agent -a start`; `make db-dump` vs `dump.mysql.hook.sh` / `dump.pgsql.hook.sh`.

**Synonyms** declare **equivalences**. They raise the hit rate of disparate recall onto a small, self-explainable set (primordial YAML; `synonym:` on entities/ables; `ASC_SYNONYMS` on make task names).

**Every step, hunt the language gap** (same loop as the rule/changelog upgrade above):

1. **NL → token.** Map redundant human words (any of the three languages) onto the English label (generic harness, not the contrib tool name), then the existing token or its synonym. Do not invent a parallel nickname or a glossary `.mdc`.
2. **Token → NL.** Reply in the language the human is using. Keep ASC tokens untranslated (or via synonym). Speak the **self-explainable token**, not the private shorthand. One name per idea.
3. If a durable name still needs a footnote: **rename it** (smallest line here and/or `changelog/`). Skip if one-off.

```text
❌ Numbered phases, weight jargon, teaching the nickname
❌ FR/EN/PT synonym table in this rule
❌ Projet Complexe language policy in this tree
❌ A per-tool pivot that does not implement the abstract ASC hook
❌ A second entity/able contract per contrib tool
✅ English as pivot NL; existing ASC synonym; or one clearer label
✅ One abstract `$subject-$action`; contrib is hook_ms specificity
```
